*.sh text eol=lf

# Certification evidence must round-trip byte-identically: the validator verifies each
# artifact's recorded SHA-256, and an LF->CRLF conversion on checkout would break every
# hash on a fresh clone. core.autocrlf is true on the certification workstation.
docs/production-certification/evidence/** -text
docs/production-certification/evidence/raw/** -text

# `agent check` compares each generated fact file byte for byte against a freshly generated
# one, and the generator writes LF. With core.autocrlf true — as it is on the Windows
# workstations this project is developed on — checkout rewrites them to CRLF and every file
# reports "stale" on a clean tree. The check then fails for a reason that has nothing to do
# with the facts being out of date, and `npm run agent -- facts` "fixes" it by writing LF
# back, leaving the tree dirty with a zero-line diff.
#
# Same reasoning as the certification evidence above: a file that is byte-compared must not
# be line-ending converted.
.agent/generated/** -text
